WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.373
Sensitive data in logs should be treated as an exposure risk.

2
00:00:03.373 --> 00:00:12.237
Obvious examples include passwords, API keys, bearer tokens, refresh
tokens, session identifiers, reset links, private keys, and full
connection strings.

3
00:00:12.237 --> 00:00:28.046
Less obvious examples include payment data, health data, government
identifiers, email addresses, phone numbers, customer-specific record
names, tenant identifiers, internal account references, file paths, and
business data that can reveal how a system or customer operates.

4
00:00:28.046 --> 00:00:32.213
The safest pattern is to avoid logging sensitive values in the first
place.

5
00:00:32.213 --> 00:00:37.240
Redaction and masking are useful controls, but they should not be the only
line of defense.

6
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:41.208
A blocklist that looks for known secret names may miss a new token field.

7
00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:44.979
A broad object dump may include nested values that nobody expected.

8
00:00:44.979 --> 00:00:48.551
An exception string from a library may include a connection URL.

9
00:00:48.551 --> 00:00:55.496
Allowlist-oriented logging is usually safer because the code chooses known
safe fields instead of copying entire objects.

10
00:00:55.496 --> 00:01:01.978
Redaction removes or replaces sensitive values before they reach logs,
traces, alerts, error reports, or tickets.

11
00:01:01.978 --> 00:01:10.445
Masking may preserve a small safe portion of a value, such as a suffix
used for support correlation, but only when that partial value is truly
acceptable.

12
00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:20.631
Hashing or tokenizing identifiers can help in some designs, but teams
should understand whether the transformed value can still be linked back
to individuals, tenants, or accounts.

13
00:01:20.631 --> 00:01:22.086
Redaction needs testing.

14
00:01:22.086 --> 00:01:28.767
It is not enough to say the logger redacts secrets if nobody has exercised
the failure paths that produce real log events.

15
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:37.366
Tests should include authentication failures, validation errors, exception
paths, dependency failures, and representative structured log records.

16
00:01:37.366 --> 00:01:44.840
Safe logging is strongest when sensitive values are excluded before
logging and redaction catches mistakes that still slip through.
